--#############################################################################
--  DIGITAL COUNTERPOINT ~ LAYER CONTROLLER  (PLUGIN)
--#############################################################################
--  Drives UCI layer visibility from a configuration grid.  Each row binds one
--  named UCI layer to an "enable" toggle (exposed as a user pin) and adds the
--  behaviours a real panel needs: radio groups, parent/child nesting, per-layer
--  in/out transitions, and STICKY radio groups.
--
--  Sticky radio groups are the reason this plugin exists.  An ordinary radio
--  group is a plain set of mutually exclusive toggles, so pressing the member
--  that is already lit turns it off and the group ends up showing NOTHING.  For
--  a navigation bar that is always wrong - one view must always be on screen.
--  Ticking Sticky makes a group refuse to empty:
--
--    * pressing the live member re-asserts it instead of clearing it;
--    * if the group is ever found empty (startup, Reset, a config edit) its
--      first row is selected automatically;
--    * hiding the group's parent does NOT clear the selection, so when the
--      parent comes back the same member is restored.
--
--  Sticky is a per-GROUP setting: the first row of a group owns the tick and
--  the remaining members mirror it (their tick greys out).  Same rule as the
--  existing Reset Group column.
--
--  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--  WORKED EXAMPLE - the nav + automix case
--  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--  The scenario this was built for (see the venue's UCI script): a
--  Zones/Mics nav pair where one view is always up, a Mic Buttons bar that must
--  stay on screen whenever Mics is selected, and a Mic Faders panel that has to
--  give way to Automix without taking the Mic Buttons bar with it.
--
--    Row  Layer Name    Logical  Radio Group  Sticky  Parent Layer
--    ---  ------------  -------  -----------  ------  ------------
--     1   Zones                  nav            X
--     2   MicsNav          X     nav            X
--     3   MicButtons                                  MicsNav
--     4   MicsFaders             micmode        X     MicsNav
--     5   Automix                micmode        X     MicsNav
--
--  MicsNav is a Logical Parent - it has no layer of its own, it just represents
--  "the Mics view is selected", so it can gate MicButtons without being subject
--  to the Automix swap.  The inner micmode group is sticky too, so exactly one
--  of MicsFaders / Automix is up whenever MicsNav is up, and neither press can
--  leave the mic area blank.  Parent Layer alone could not express this: the
--  nav pair could be turned off entirely, and parenting MicButtons to the
--  MicsFaders layer meant engaging Automix hid the bar as well.
--
--  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--  PIN ACCESS
--  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--  v0.9.1 adds the panel lock.  It used to live in the venue's Text Controller
--  as a Lua variable and a table of PIN-to-layer rows, which meant the one
--  thing an operator most wants to see - who is allowed what - was readable
--  only by opening a script.  It is now three more columns in this grid and a
--  handful of pins.
--
--  The pad is the stock Q-SYS PIN Pad.  Its pin.match.0..N-1 booleans reach
--  this plugin's Pin Matched inputs and its logout trigger reaches Lock.  The
--  plugin then holds which code is live (Pin Active), reports whether the panel
--  is open (Unlocked), and gates every layer accordingly.
--
--  ONE ROUTE, NOT TWO.  Either wire those pins in the design OR let
--  UCITextController_V1.6.lua mirror them - it already holds a proxy for the
--  pad and does exactly this - but not both, or two writers share one input.
--  The designs this was written for use the script.
--
--  AND LEAVE DimTSC ON THE PAD.  Everything that locks the panel funnels
--  through the pad's own logout trigger, which is what also clears its match
--  state.  Wiring the dim straight to this plugin's Lock pin instead would lock
--  the layers while leaving the pad believing it was still logged in - and one
--  Enter on an empty code would let the next person straight back in.
--
--  Three kinds of permission column:
--
--    Default  what you get when the page loads, and what is allowed while the
--             panel is LOCKED.  THE ONLY column that is a STATE as well as a
--             permission - entering it writes the enables to match, which is
--             what puts the login screen back on a logout.
--    PIN Off  what a panel with Pin Enabled switched off may reach.  A
--             PERMISSION MASK and nothing else, as of v0.9.7.
--    PIN n    the same, for while code n is the live one.
--
--  A permission mask FORCES DOWN a layer it does not tick - not by its button,
--  not by a radio group, not by a script writing its enable pin.  A layer it
--  DOES tick is merely allowed; something still has to raise it.
--
--  PIN OFF STOPPED SEEDING IN v0.9.7, and the reason is worth keeping.  A
--  permission column is ticked with everything the role may REACH, and a state
--  column with the much smaller set that should be ON SCREEN.  While PIN Off
--  was both, ticking it honestly - every menu, sub-menu, source detail and mic
--  layout a lock-free panel can get to - raised the lot at once the moment the
--  lock was disarmed or the Core came up.  Ticking it narrowly instead made
--  those layers unreachable for the rest of the session.  There was no third
--  answer, so the two jobs were split: Default states, everything else
--  permits.  With PIN Off ticked and not seeding, the login layer masks itself
--  off for free, because PIN Off does not tick it.
--
--  What supplies the state instead: the enables Q-SYS persisted (adopted at
--  startup, so a Core comes back where it was) and the Reassert pulse, which
--  asks whatever owns the layers to say again what it wants.
--
--  That split is deliberate.  A PIN column that also raised its layers would
--  be a second, invisible writer on every enable pin, racing whatever else
--  drives them.  Permission composes instead: the venue's script goes on
--  writing the layers its mode wants, and the plugin refuses the ones this
--  login is not entitled to.
--
--  REFUSED IS NOT FORGOTTEN (v0.9.5).  A write the live column will not allow
--  still records its request; only the enable pin is snapped back.  So the
--  script states what the venue wants whenever it likes, and each layer comes
--  up the moment a column that permits it goes live.  Before v0.9.5 the
--  request was dropped, which made the Reassert pulse the ONLY way anything
--  script-owned came back after a login - one round-trip, no memory behind it.
--
--  A consequence worth knowing when reading a grid: a row nothing seeds and
--  nothing external ever writes is never requested, so ticking it in PIN 1..n
--  alone raises it never.  That bites hardest on a LOGICAL PARENT, which draws
--  nothing itself and so gives no clue: its children read enable true and
--  visible false, on a panel that looks correctly configured.  Give a gate
--  like that a Default tick - it is free, because a logical row draws nothing
--  on the locked panel either.
--
--  AN EMPTY COLUMN is an unconfigured one, and the two cases want opposite
--  answers.  PIN Off fails OPEN - Pin Enabled starts false, so a v0.9 design
--  opens in v0.9.1 and behaves exactly as it did until the first tick goes in.
--  Default and every PIN column fail CLOSED, because they are the locked state
--  and the per-code grants of a security feature and a blank one that permitted
--  everything would be a lock that had quietly stopped locking.  Status names
--  the offending column rather than leaving a dark panel unexplained.  Either
--  way an empty column SEEDS NOTHING, so it can never wipe a panel's state.
--
--  REDUCING Number of PIN Codes destroys the PIN columns above the new count
--  and their ticks go with them, the same way reducing Number of Layers loses
--  its rows.  Raising it again gives you blank columns, which fail closed.
--
--  Sticky is what turns a widened mask back into a visible layer.  A sticky
--  group whose selection has just been forbidden is an empty group, and an
--  empty sticky group selects its first PERMITTED member - so a Login / Lock
--  pair with Login ticked in Default and Lock ticked in every PIN column swaps
--  itself on login and swaps back on logout, with no per-PIN state anywhere.
--
--  Pin Matched is read on its RISING EDGE ONLY, never its level.  The stock
--  pad's documentation does not say whether pin.match.N latches after a match
--  or merely pulses, and on an edge it does not matter: the edge happens once
--  per login either way, a level left latched across a logout raises no new
--  edge, and a stale one therefore cannot hand its layers to the next person
--  who logs in on an ordinary code.
--
--  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--  NOTES
--  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--  * Transitions are per row - a layer always animates in with its own
--    Transition In and out with its own Transition Out, including when it is
--    shown or hidden as a side effect of a parent change.
--  * Visibility is computed declaratively (a layer is visible when its enable
--    is on AND every ancestor is visible) and then diffed against what was last
--    pushed, so each Uci.SetLayerVisibility call happens only on a real change.
--    Parent cycles are rejected at config time rather than trapped at runtime.
--  * Leave a layer out of any Radio Group / Parent if an external script drives
--    its enable pin directly, or the two will fight.
--
--  Revisions
--    v0.9.10 UCI Name is a user pin (I/O), so the name can be driven from
--          whatever already holds it instead of being typed a second time.
--    v0.9.9  A Reset Group snap pulses Reassert.  It overwrites the requests
--          of a whole group, which is exactly what that output is for, and
--          leaving it silent meant nothing could recover the selection.
--    v0.9.8  Apply no longer publishes over an enable pin holding a write
--          nobody has read yet, so a batch of cross-component writes survives
--          instead of collapsing to whichever one arrived first.
--    v0.9.7  PIN Off is PERMISSION ONLY - it no longer raises what it ticks,
--          so it can be filled in as the set a lock-free panel may reach
--          without that set arriving all at once.  Default is now the only
--          column that is a state as well as a permission.  A config reload
--          pulses Reassert, so an edit reaches the scripts that own layers.
--    v0.9.6  Status names a column that permits a layer but not its parent -
--          the grid fault that shows up as a dark panel and nothing else.
--    v0.9.5  A request refused by the live permission column is REMEMBERED
--          rather than discarded, so a layer comes up the moment a column
--          that permits it goes live instead of waiting on Reassert.
--    v0.9.4  Publishes the reliable Reassert correction as a new plugin
--          version so Q-SYS Designer recognises the in-place upgrade.
--    v0.9.3  Fires the Reassert output with Control:Trigger(), so the pulse
--          reaches external scripts on a real Core after permission changes.
--    v0.9.2  Preserves requested layer state when Reset uses an unconfigured
--          PIN Off column; widens and derives the configuration-grid layout.
--    v0.9.1  PIN access.  Number of PIN Codes property; Pin Enabled, Pin
--          Matched (input) / Pin Active (LED) / Unlocked (LED) and a Lock
--          trigger; Default, PIN Off and PIN 1..N permission columns; every
--          enable masked by the live column; sticky groups fall back to their
--          first PERMITTED member.  Absorbs the lock that used to live in
--          UCITextController - see UCITextController_V1.6.lua.
--    v0.9  First release.  Replaces the Advanced UCI Controller in the
--          designs it was written for, keeping its control names so a commissioned
--          venue upgrades in place.  New: sticky radio groups, cycle-checked
--          parent nesting, declarative visibility diffing.
--
--  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--  ORIGIN AND LICENCE
--  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
